      "math_academy_id": "e-695",
      "problem": "Write $0.1\\overline{4}$ as geometric series.",
      "worked_solution": "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =0.144\\,4... \\\\  & =0.1+0.04+0.004+0.000\\,4+… \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+(\\frac{4}{100}+\\frac{4}{1000}+\\frac{4}{10\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term $a_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}$ and the common ratio $r=\\dfrac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4\\cdot 10}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
